** ==============================================================================
** ABAQUS/STANDARD THERMO-METALLURGICAL MATERIAL CARD: EN-GJS-600-10 (5.3110)
** Solid-solution strengthened ferritic spheroidal graphite cast iron
** Typical composition 3.2 C - 4.2 Si - 0.25 Mn (wt%), ferritic matrix
** Units: mm, s, tonne, mJ, MPa, C
** Author: Victor Maia (vhfm08@gmail.com)
**
** ESTIMATED DATA, FIRST APPROXIMATION. No measurement of this iron is behind this
** card. It keeps the four phases and the layout of the 16MnCr5 card (smooth
** curves, plateaus above the liquidus, liquid-state strength floor) so that both
** materials run in the same model, with the values below.
**
** Phases as used here:
**   Ferrite    = as-cast ferritic matrix with graphite nodules (base material)
**   Austenite  = matrix austenite, about 0.5 % C from graphite dissolution
**   Bainite    = ausferrite / bainite formed from that austenite
**   Martensite = high-carbon martensite of the heat affected zone
** Not represented: ledeburite (white iron) of the partially melted zone, the
** carbon gradient around the nodules, and the weld metal of the filler wire.
** The fusion zone solidifies as austenite and ends as martensite in this card.
**
** Values and their origin:
**  - Solidus 1150 C, liquidus 1220 C: eutectic range of a 4.2 % Si iron
**    (stable eutectic about 1154 + 6.5 Si), widened to 70 K for a smooth latent
**    heat release. Latent heat 230 kJ/kg (handbook range for cast iron).
**  - Density 7.10, E = 170 GPa, nu = 0.275, Rp0.2 = 470 MPa, Rm = 600 MPa:
**    EN 1563 and the room temperature card of project 01.
**  - Specific heat: steel values up to 600 C; the mean 20-500 C value is
**    518 J/kg.K (EN 1563: 515). The magnetic (Curie) peak, about 770 J/kg.K, is on
**    the ferrite only, at 730 C (silicon lowers the Curie temperature of iron
**    slightly, it does not follow Ac1). Austenite is paramagnetic and has a smooth
**    curve; the two curves meet at Ac3 (650 J/kg.K at 970 C), so the ferrite ->
**    austenite switch does not cause a jump in heat capacity.
**  - Thermal expansion: steel curves x 0.94 (EN 1563: 12.5E-6 1/K for 20-400 C).
**  - Conductivity: 27 to 28 W/m.K up to 600 C, falling to 23.5 near 950 C.
**    Estimate for 4 % Si ferrite; EN 1563 gives 36.2 for the 2.5 % Si ferritic
**    grades at 300 C and silicon lowers it. To be replaced by a measured value.
**  - Ferrite yield stress versus temperature: 1.00 / 0.82 / 0.62 / 0.38 / 0.20 /
**    0.10 of the room temperature value at 20 / 400 / 500 / 600 / 700 / 800 C,
**    from published tests of ferritic and SiMo ductile irons (stable to about
**    400 C, about 20 % at 700 C and 10 % at 800 C).
**  - Bainite and martensite flow curves: steel curves raised to 800 and 1500 MPa
**    yield at 20 C; the martensite stress is capped at 2400 MPa.
**  - Ac1 = 850 C, Ac3 = 970 C on heating: silicon raises the eutectoid range
**    (about 730 + 28 Si for the lower limit; calculations in the literature for
**    4 % Si irons give 840 to 906 C and 900 to 990 C).
**  - Ms = 290 C: Andrews-type formula with 0.5 % C and 4.2 % Si in the austenite.
**  - Austenite -> ferrite and -> bainite: Li et al. (1998) reaction-rate model
**    with 0.5 C - 4.2 Si - 0.25 Mn, outside its calibration range in silicon.
**  - Ferrite -> austenite: ten times slower than in the steel card, because the
**    carbon has to come from the graphite nodules. Assumed, not measured.
**    All heating transformations end at Ac3 = 970 C. Extra points between
**    1050 and 1150 C follow a log-linear interpolation of the times, because
**    Abaqus interpolates the tables linearly.
**
** Not modelled: the enthalpy of the solid-state transformations. The framework
** has no input for it; a HETVAL subroutine would be needed.
**
** References:
**  - EN 1563:2018, Founding - Spheroidal graphite cast irons
**  - Li, Niebuhr, Meekisho, Atteridge, Metall. Mater. Trans. B 29 (1998) 661-672
**  - Andrews, J. Iron Steel Inst. 203 (1965) 721-727
**  - Koistinen, Marburger, Acta Metall. 7 (1959) 59-60
**  - Roucka, Abramova, Kana, Arch. Metall. Mater. 63 (2018) 601-607
**  - Damage analysis of a ferritic SiMo ductile cast iron in temperature,
**    Metals 5 (2015) 2351, doi:10.3390/met5042351
**
** The table type definitions are in Material_phase_trans_table_types.inp, which
** must be included once in the model.
** ==============================================================================
